load @cluesurf/form/code/structure/set
  find set

load @cluesurf/form/code/structure/manifold
  find manifold

# The boundary at infinity, the ideal sphere the hyperbolic space approaches but never reaches, and the geometry that
# lives on it. In the substrate this boundary is the thin skin of the baseball, the cusp where the dense bulk meets
# physical reality.

# The ideal boundary: the set of ideal points, one for each "end" a geodesic can run out to, forming a sphere one
# dimension below the space (a circle for the plane, a two-sphere for hyperbolic three-space). It is not part of the
# space (no point is at finite distance from the interior) yet it organizes everything: geodesics are pinned by their
# two ideal endpoints, and the isometries act on it by conformal (Mobius) maps. The form carries the set of ideal
# points.
form ideal-boundary
  link points, like set

# Ideal, hyperideal, and ordinary vertices: a tiling vertex may be an ordinary interior point (giving a COMPACT tiling),
# an ideal point on the boundary (PARACOMPACT, the cusp case), or a hyperideal point outside the space entirely
# (NON-COMPACT). The substrate's honeycomb and the pentagrid sit in this family, and the cusp where the mesh meets the
# skin is an ideal vertex. The form carries the boundary the vertex type is measured against.
form vertex-type
  link boundary, like set

# Sphere packing and the limit set: a hyperbolic Coxeter group of level two leaves a maximal sphere packing on the
# ideal boundary, spheres filling the boundary without overlapping, whose complement is the limit set (the closure of
# the group's orbit). Level one fills the whole boundary with the limit set (no packing); level above two forces the
# spheres to overlap. This (Maxwell's level classification) is the boundary-side fingerprint of which hyperbolic tiling
# a Coxeter group makes. The form carries the boundary the packing lives on.
form sphere-packing
  link boundary, like set
